# The reviewer /ship runs for step 6, the pull request's code review. It is a
# command line; the review prompt is appended to it as one argument.
#
# In a separate gate it reads for correctness and a second reviewer runs the
# security pass. In a combined gate it is the only review the branch gets, and
# the prompt it is given covers the security areas as well. The gate says which
# mode it ran in, in the pull request body, either way.
#
# `auto` means the command is chosen when the gate runs, from what this host
# has: codex when it is on PATH, otherwise Claude Code with Fable in plan mode,
# which reads the tree and the diff and can change neither. Either way the
# command is told not to read the reviewed repository's own AGENTS.md or
# CLAUDE.md, which would otherwise let the change under review write part of its
# reviewer's instructions. A command line you write yourself is not given that
# treatment; it is yours, and isolating it is yours too. With neither codex nor
# claude on the host the gate stops and names your file. The choice is made
# again on every run and never written down, so a machine that installs codex
# later starts using it without an edit anywhere.
#
# This file is replaced whenever the skill is updated, so do not edit it. To
# change the reviewer, copy it to ~/.config/ship/reviewer, or to
# $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ship/reviewer when that is set, and edit the copy: /ship reads
# that file first and falls back to this one. A value you write there is never
# probed.
#
# If the model named here is refused, fall back to another one and say in the
# pull request which reviewer actually ran. A review that silently downgraded is
# worse than one that did not happen.
auto
